<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudolf II of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 880–<a href="/years/937">937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Rudolf II of Upper Burgundy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudolf II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> from 912 to 937. Defeated by the Swabians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> in 919, he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and was crowned king there in 922, but was driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles">Hugh of Arles</a>. Around 933 Hugh gave up his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a> in exchange, and Rudolf united the two Burgundian kingdoms into what became the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Rudolf II of Burgundy" width="320" height="553" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Universitätsbibliothek Freiburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudolph_Burgundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>A member of the Elder House of Welf, Rudolph was the son of the Upper Burgundian king Rudolph I and his wife Guilla of Provence. After he came to the throne in 912, he entered a border conflict with the neighbouring dukes of Swabia and campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> and Zürich estates. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-duke-of-swabia">Burchard II</a> of Swabia defeated him at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> in 919. The two rulers then made peace, and in 922 Rudolph married Burchard's daughter Bertha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the same time several Italian nobles, led by Margrave Adalbert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a>, asked Rudolph to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against Emperor Berengar. Having entered Italy, he was crowned king at Pavia in the Basilica of San Michele Maggiore. On 17 July 923 he defeated Berengar at the Battle of Fiorenzuola. Berengar was murdered the following year, possibly at Rudolph's instigation. The king then ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> and Italy together, residing alternately in both kingdoms.</p><p>In 926 the Italian nobility turned against him and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles">Hugh of Arles</a>, the regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>, to rule them instead. Rudolph's father-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-duke-of-swabia">Burchard II</a>, came to support him but was attacked and killed near Novara by followers of Lambert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The king returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> to protect himself, which assured Hugh's coronation as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Rudolph gave the Holy Lance to the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> in exchange for estates at Basel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, in either 926 or 935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind">Louis the Blind</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a> died in 928, Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Rudolph's rival, inherited that realm. To secure his position in Italy, Hugh agreed to cede his claims to Lower Burgundy in exchange for Rudolph's renunciation of any Italian claims. The two Burgundian realms were unified from 933 under Rudolph, and the united polity was later known as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>. He died in July 937 and was succeeded by his son Conrad.</p><p>After his death his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> was married to Hugh's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, while Hugh married Rudolph's widow Bertha. Adelaide later became the second wife of Otto the Great, crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> in 962, and the mother of Emperor Otto II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudolph II of Burgundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hugh of Arles, busy with his ambitions as king of Italy, gave up his claims to <b>Rudolf II of Upper Burgundy</b> around 933.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lower-burgundy">Kingdom of Lower Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rudolf II</b> was defeated by Swabia at Winterthur in 919, then reached for the Italian crown in the 920s before trading those claims for Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
